  <p>A frantic freelance video editor recently contacted me with a perplexing issue: "I shot my commercial project in raw 4K resolution, yet after export, the video looks heavily compressed—like it was re-encoded over WhatsApp." When we opened his delivery configuration in DaVinci Resolve, his rendering resolution was locked correctly to 3840×2160 UHD. The destructive culprit lay elsewhere: <strong>his maximum target bitrate was restricted to merely 8 Mbps.</strong> While camera resolution merely defines how many pixels exist inside your video frame grid, your bitrate defines precisely how much computational data is assigned to describe what exists within those pixels. Without adequate bitrate data, even four million ultra-high-definition pixels will look blocky, empty, and degraded.</p>

