  <h2 id="codec">How Codec Efficiency Impacts Target Bitrate</h2>
  <p>Never evaluate target bitrate figures in isolation; your selected video compression codec plays an equally important role in determining overall image preservation. Modern HEVC (H.265) encoding algorithms offer dramatic computational advancements over older legacy AVC (H.264) frameworks—delivering equivalent visual perceived quality at roughly half the total bitrate bandwidth. Therefore, an H.265 video export running at 10 Mbps will display sharper structural preservation than an H.264 file running at identical data rates. This improved compression explains why cutting-edge streaming services increasingly adopt H.265 or open-source AV1 codecs to conserve cloud bandwidth without compromising image quality.</p>
  <p>However, advanced encoding frameworks carry clear processing trade-offs: computing and decoding H.265 and AV1 streams demands significant hardware graphical acceleration from target viewing devices. While modern smartphones and workstations navigate H.265 effortlessly, legacy laptops and older mobile processors frequently drop frames when attempting to decode complex HEVC media. Select H.264 when maximum broad ecosystem compatibility is paramount, and select H.265 when prioritizing minimized storage dimensions and rapid file uploads.</p>

  <h2 id="math">Calculating Video File Size From Bitrate</h2>
  <p>Mastering basic bitrate file sizing mathematics empowers professional video editors to manage local storage drives effectively before arriving on set. To accurately compute estimated project file size in megabytes (MB), employ this formula: <strong>Total File Size (MB) = [Target Bitrate (Mbps) × Clip Duration (in seconds)] ÷ 8</strong>. We divide by eight because network transfer bitrates operate in bits, whereas hard storage drives measure capacity in Bytes (where 1 Byte equals 8 individual bits).</p>
  <p>Consider a practical field scenario: recording ten minutes (600 seconds) of continuous interview footage at a robust 45 Mbps target data rate. Your mathematical calculation resolves as follows: 45 Mbps multiplied by 600 seconds equals 27,000 Megabits. Dividing 27,000 by 8 yields an exact physical file profile of 3,375 Megabytes—or roughly <strong>3.37 Gigabytes</strong> per 10-minute take. Extending this equation across eight hours of two-camera corporate interview recording highlights why commercial documentary editors invest heavily in multi-terabyte SSD enclosures and redundant cloud archival arrays.</p>

